import
	../../html

intro-global html[]()
	- section "Direct global side effects",
		- p quote
			Global side effects aren't allowed in ordinary functions. (Though {fn-log} is allowed.)
			{br}
			That means we still haven't done a proper "Hello, world"!

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/io/print

			main void() global
				print "Hello, world!"

		- p quote
			Any function like {fn-print} that directly has a global side effect must be marked with the {global_} spec.
			{br}
			Even {em "observing"} global state, such as reading a file, is considered a global side effect.

	- section "Global side effects through a lambda",
		- p "A function without the {global_} spec can still call lambdas that have global side effects."

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/io/print

			main void() global
				do-times 10, of
					print "Hello, world!"

			do-times void(times nat, f void mut())
				for _ in 0 .. times
					f[]

		- p quote
			Notice that while a global side effect happened {em "during"} {function "do-times"},
			{function "do-times"} itself does nto have global side effects.
			So it does not have to be marked {global_}.

		- p "{em "Creating"} a lambda that does {global_} things is {global_}, but {em "calling"} the lambda is not."

		- p "A {data_} lambda can not have global side effects."

	- section "Global side effects through an interface",
		- p "A similar thing can be done with interfaces."

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/io/print

			main void() global
				print-times 10, my-printer

			print-times void(times nat, printer printer)
				for _ in 0 .. times
					print printer, "hello"

			printer interface mut
				print void(message string)
			my-printer record global, mut, printer case
			print void(_ my-printer, message string) global
				print message

		- p quote
			Notice how {type "my-printer"} had to be marked as {global_}.
			{br}
			Marking a {record_} as {global_} makes its {fn-new}
			(and named {function "my-printer"}) functions {global_} as well,
			so you can only construct a {type "my-printer"} in {global_} code.
			{br}
			If a record is {global_}, it can implement an interface using {global_} functions.
		- p quote
			The interface needs to be {mut_} for this to work, since {data_} types can't have side effects.

	- section "Trusting global side effects",
		- p quote
			{trusted_} allows its argument to call {global_} (in addition to {unsafe}) functions.
			{br}
			Do this only if you know that the side effects are harmless.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/io/print

			main void()
				trusted print "This is allowed"

	- section "Global variables",
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void() global, unsafe
				log x
				x := 2
				log x

			x global(nat)
				1 # This is the initial value

		- aside-small quote
			Accessing a global variable both has a global side effect and can violate thread safety.
			{br}
			So it is both {global_} and {unsafe}.
